      diveDeep: 'Permeability describes how quickly water can flow through a material. It depends on the size and connectivity of pore spaces. Coarser particles have larger, more connected pores. Fine clay particles are tiny and pack together tightly, creating very small pores that greatly impede water flow — clay is nearly impermeable. Porosity (total pore volume) and permeability are related but different properties: a material can be porous but not permeable if the pores are disconnected. Sorted sediments (uniform size) generally have higher permeability than unsorted mixtures for the same particle size.',
