      image: '/images/exams/es-august-2018/context_48_50.png',
      explanation: 'By the law of superposition, the lowest undisturbed sedimentary layer (F) was deposited first, before any of the layers above it or the intrusion and faulting events.',
      diveDeep: 'Relative dating uses several principles to determine the sequence of geologic events: (1) Superposition — lower layers are older; (2) Original horizontality — layers began flat; (3) Crosscutting relationships — intrusions and faults are younger than the rocks they cut through; (4) Inclusions — fragments within a rock are older than the rock containing them. On any cross section, establish the sequence by first identifying the oldest layer (lowest undisturbed), then the sequence of deposition, then any intrusions or faults (which must post-date the rocks they affect). Practice this sequence with multiple cross sections.',
