      diveDeep: 'Crosscutting relationships are one of the most reliable tools for relative dating. If magma intrudes (cuts through) existing rock layers, it must have been injected after those layers formed. The intruded layers do not change the intrusion — it is the intrusion that disrupts the layers. Similarly, a fault that cuts across rock layers must have occurred after those layers were deposited. Contact metamorphism zones (baked/altered rock) adjacent to intrusions also indicate younger intrusive events. This principle applies to any feature that physically cuts across or through other features.',
