      explanation: 'Contact metamorphism occurs when heat from a magma intrusion bakes the surrounding rock, changing its mineral structure and creating metamorphic rock in a zone adjacent to the intrusion.',
      diveDeep: 'Contact metamorphism is a localized (small-scale) type of metamorphism caused by heat from an igneous intrusion. The zone of altered rock around an intrusion is called a contact metamorphic aureole. The rock type produced depends on what the surrounding (country) rock was: limestone → marble; shale → hornfels; sandstone → quartzite. Importantly, the intrusion itself is igneous, not metamorphic. The larger the intrusion, the wider the contact metamorphic zone. This is how hornfels forms — one of the few metamorphic rocks produced only by contact (heat alone, not pressure) metamorphism.',
